Expected: six lines with the role's App identity on a worker pane; the same grep on the root architect's and controller's pane pids prints nothing. A commit made from inside the worker pane (its own phase's `jj split`) shows that identity as author and committer in `jj log`. Negative control for the E2E line: the same grep on the controller pane (prints nothing) — a deliberately identity-less pane.

The reviewer verifies the identity of this PR's own commits (author and committer per role, from `legion gh -- pr view --json commits` or `jj log`) as an incidental check: after the daemon deploys on this change, every Legion commit is direct evidence.

## Rollout notes

- No `LEGION_DAEMON_API_VERSION` bump: no request/response shape changes.
- The daemon change alone is the fix: with `JJ_USER`/`JJ_EMAIL` on the pane, a pre-upgrade plugin's `jj config set --repo` at boot no longer affects any commit (env wins), and provisioning removes what it wrote at the next launch. The plugin release (Task 3) is hygiene and lands with the same release train as usual.
- Existing panes at the time of the daemon restart keep their old environment (no identity variables) until their next `--resume` relaunch; their commits fall back to whatever config says until then — the exact window the one-time unset makes harmless (no repo-scoped identity left; the user config's identity applies, and the reviewer's identity check flags it).

## Concerns (for the architect)

1. **`packages/workspace` has no CI job.** `pr-and-main.yaml` runs `packages/daemon` only; `envoy-and-contracts.yaml` runs contracts, envoy-plugin, envoy-client, pi-envoy, dispatch, claude-envoy-bridge. Task 2's tests are gated by the implementer's and tester's local `bun test` runs, recorded in the PR body. LEGION-70's plan proposed adding the job; if it has merged by implementation time, this branch inherits it — the implementer checks `.github/workflows` before writing the PR body.
2. **Out of scope, observed while planning (not changed here):** (a) `catchup.ts`'s `MODE_TO_ROLE` maps planner/tester/architect catch-up *reads* to the review App, while `appRoleForLegionRole` (the identity and credential mapping this issue uses, as the spec directs) maps them to the implement App — a pre-existing inconsistency in a read-only path. (b) `workspace.ts:204-213`'s recovery path runs `jj workspace forget <name> --cleanup --force`; neither jj 0.45.1 (fork) nor stock 0.44.0 on this box accepts `--cleanup`/`--force` (`jj workspace forget --help` lists neither), so that branch would fail with an argument error if ever reached. (c) `scripts/smoke/checkpoints.sh:303` asserts `implementer+ses_` identity and a `Legion-Session:` trailer, and `:346-348` asserts the reviewer App authored the `.legion` deletion — all three predate the current design (App-identity emails, `Omp-Session`, implementer pushes the deletion).
3. Tests that seed a repo-scoped key on a real jj (Task 2 Step 4, Task 3 Step 1) leave an empty `~/.config/jj/repos/<hash>/config.toml` for the temp repo behind — both jj builds store repo config there. Same footprint as the test being replaced; noted, not fixed.

## Workflow recommendation

One implementer, tasks in order 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5, TDD per task (write the test, watch the named failure, implement, watch it pass), one `jj split` commit per task with only the named paths, one pull request. Tasks 1–3 are independent in code but Task 2 also edits `processes.test.ts` (the exact provisioning list), so keep the order to avoid a same-file merge. The tester runs the criterion-3 recipe and the real-pane environ check; the reviewer checks every acceptance row against the inventory table and the six-variable contract against the diff.
